<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Can I Have a Pencil?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify written instructions for peers. Communication: Relay simplified explanations of lessons to absent peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify written instructions for peers using basic words and gestures (e.g., points to a pencil and says, "Ask for a pencil."). Can relay simple, polite request phrases to absent peers by acting them out when they return (e.g., mimes "Can I have this, please?"). Can rephrase polite request phrases using simple words (e.g., "Can I have this?" becomes "Please, pencil.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a picture of two students at a desk. One student looks sad because they don't have a pencil, and the other student has many. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" "What does the sad student need?" "How can the sad student ask for a pencil?" The teacher elicits "Can I have a pencil, please?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the phrase "Can I have a pencil, please?" clearly, emphasizing the /p/ sound in "please". The teacher also models how to simplify this by pointing to a pencil and saying "Pencil, please!" with a kind gesture. The teacher explains that sometimes we need to help friends understand in a simple way, like if they were absent yesterday.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a friend needs a pencil, what can you say?" (Expected: "Can I have a pencil, please?")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend was absent and doesn't know how to ask, how can you help them understand? (Show 'Pencil, please!' with a gesture)." (Expected: Simple words, gestures.)</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple "Polite Request Poster" example. It includes an image of a hand offering a pencil and the text: "Can I have a pencil, please?" and another image of a student rolling dice with the text "I can't roll the dice. Can you help me?" Below these, there are simpler explanations like "Pencil, please!" and "Help me, please!" with accompanying gestures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will be able to make polite requests and offer assistance politely using basic vocabulary and simple sentence structures (e.g., "Can I have...please?", "Can I help you?").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to "Can I have a pencil, please?" and asks, "What does this mean?" Then points to "Pencil, please!" and asks, "Is this easier to understand?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "When we say 'I can't roll the dice', does it sound polite?" (Focus on /k/ sound in 'can't'). Then, "How can we help a friend understand 'I can't'?" (Demonstrate "No, help me!" with a shrug).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice rephrasing polite requests and simple instructions using polite requests ("Can I have...please?", "Can I help you?"), negative contracted forms ("I can't"), and key vocabulary (pencil, desk, friend, ask, need, help, please, thank you).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Simplify It! (Controlled Practice)</b>
                    The teacher writes simple requests on the board (e.g., "I need a pencil.", "Give me paper."). Students work in groups to rephrase them using "Can I have...please?" (e.g., "Can I have a pencil, please?"). Then, they simplify them further (e.g., "Pencil, please!"). The teacher checks for correct usage of "please" and the /p/ sound.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Act It Out! (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill)</b>
                    The teacher provides sentence frames: "Can I have a _____, please?" and "I can't _____. Can you help?" The teacher shows flashcards (e.g., "eraser", "book", "roll the dice") and students substitute the word into the frame, then practice simplifying the request or explanation using gestures and 1-2 words (e.g., for "roll the dice": "Roll! No! Help me!"). Students focus on the /k/ sound in "can't".</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Relay the Message (Pair Interaction)</b>
                    Students work in pairs. Student A gets a card with a polite request (e.g., "Can I borrow your book, please?"). Student B pretends to be an absent friend who just returned. Student A must relay the message to Student B using simplified language and gestures (e.g., pointing to the book, saying "Book, please!"). Student B repeats the simplified message. They switch roles.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group presentation of "Polite Request Poster" with an emphasis on mediation. Students will present their Project 1 posters to the class, specifically focusing on *explaining* and *simplifying* the requests for peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, in pre-assigned small groups, display their "Polite Request Posters" (created in previous lessons or as homework).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group takes a turn to present their poster. Their main task is to explain ONE polite request from their poster to the class, pretending some classmates were absent and need a simple explanation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must state the polite request (e.g., "Can I have a pencil, please?"), then rephrase it using simpler words and gestures (e.g., pointing to the pencil on the poster and saying, "This means 'Pencil, please!'").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should also explain a polite response, such as "Sorry, I can't help." and simplify it if possible (e.g., "No, sorry!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages other students to ask simple questions for clarification, which the presenting group must simplify in response.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations and individual student self-assessment via exit ticket.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their Project 1: Polite Request Poster and explain one polite request from it, simplifying the message for their peers using words and gestures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key words (e.g., /p/ in "please", /k/ in "can't").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., pencil, friend, ask, help, please, thank you).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of rephrasing polite requests into simpler words (e.g., "Can I have a pencil, please?" becomes "Pencil, please!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures to relay simplified messages.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration within the group during presentation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can simplify 'Can I have a pencil, please?' to __________."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is important to simplify messages for my friends because..."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Connecting learning and planning for future lessons.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining your poster to a friend?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult about simplifying the words?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well? (e.g., Most students were able to simplify requests using basic vocabulary and gestures. The poster project provided a concrete context for mediation.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with the "I can't" pronunciation and simplifying negative expressions. Future lessons could include more explicit practice with mediating responses of inability.)</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "A Polite Request." The next theme will build on basic classroom interactions by introducing new vocabulary for school activities and more complex simple present tense structures, continuing to emphasize polite communication in different contexts. The skills of mediating information will be useful in relaying instructions for new activities.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>